{:edb/db-before-t 397 :edb/db-after-t 398 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533710 50 #inst "2026-10-04T18:03:56.762Z" 13194139533710 true] [17592186110526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247892 13194139533710 true] [17592186110526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247899 13194139533710 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247885 13194139533710 true] [17592186110527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247889 13194139533710 true] [17592186110528 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247878 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247879 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1089 "q-110139" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1100 "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=-1$ and passes through the point $(-2,-3).$" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1113 #uuid "dda0082e-1a67-5132-baf3-4657026b2294" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1138 false 13194139533710 true] [17592186247878 1145 "Since the line $x=-1$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(-2,-3),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $-3.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=-3.$" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247880 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247881 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247882 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247883 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247884 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1041 "selection" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247881 13194139533710 true] [17592186247879 1110 #uuid "1a8b5cec-a9ad-5c1a-831f-8ed990e5ba82" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247880 1034 "x=-3" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247880 1111 #uuid "5da72cd1-7d87-5238-9d17-b976c2e690cf" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247881 1034 "y=-3" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247881 1111 #uuid "1fb06c44-61f8-554a-b3ce-d936b9ca16c2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247882 1034 "x=-2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247882 1111 #uuid "ccb2bc96-5190-5dca-bf14-b231262046dc" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247883 1034 "y=-2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247883 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247883 1111 #uuid "109a639d-5e3b-5cce-9c8d-d52a9bde8791" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247884 1034 "y=1" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247884 1111 #uuid "073607aa-afba-52a6-8a14-a38936e24cd2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247886 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1089 "q-215942" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1100 "The lines $y=7x-2$ and $kx-14y=7$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1113 #uuid "a0db3a7c-dcad-567a-a447-ae16f102a4f0" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1138 false 13194139533710 true] [17592186247885 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=7x-2$ is $7.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-14y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-14y & =7 \\\\ 14y & =kx-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{14}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-14y=7$ is $\\frac{k}{14}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (\\frac{k}{14})=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247887 13194139533710 true] [17592186247886 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247888 13194139533710 true] [17592186247886 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533710 true] [17592186247886 1041 "field-1" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247886 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247888 13194139533710 true] [17592186247886 1110 #uuid "9c60144b-fd74-523f-a736-9b850a3a9483" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247887 1034 "2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247887 1111 #uuid "0d952d48-fcc2-5df5-8571-e5f4eece4465" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247888 1034 "-2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247888 1111 #uuid "0a02461e-5b95-5d8e-acde-915806813e8d" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247890 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1089 "q-215944" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1100 "The lines $10x+5y=3$ and $2kx+y=2$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1113 #uuid "91882045-b011-58c7-bd7c-d2cb4bd977bd" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1138 false 13194139533710 true] [17592186247889 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $10x+5y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x+5y & =3 \\\\ 5y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-2x+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $2kx+y=2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2kx+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2kx+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $10x+5y=3$ is $-2.$\n\n- The slope of the line $2kx+y=2$ is $-2k.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot (-2k)=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247891 13194139533710 true] [17592186247890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533710 true] [17592186247890 1041 "field-1" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247891 13194139533710 true] [17592186247890 1110 #uuid "347c33ae-0e99-5f5d-8b04-920adc5cefd4" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247891 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247891 1111 #uuid "5100e8d7-acb8-5055-bd05-6c09f40ddf3f" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247893 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1089 "q-23378" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1100 "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $5x-y=7?$" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1113 #uuid "5e5c5991-22ca-5c45-9c80-3f1c166c4c5e" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1138 false 13194139533710 true] [17592186247892 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $5x-y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-y & =7 \\\\ -y & =-5x+7 \\\\ y & =5x-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $5.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x-6$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x-6$ is perpendicular to the given line." 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247894 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247895 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247896 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247897 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247898 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1041 "selection" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247894 13194139533710 true] [17592186247893 1110 #uuid "348882ca-3d6a-5800-be28-1bdd49d1ec74" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247894 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{5}x-6" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247894 1111 #uuid "fb197ae3-2554-5663-98ec-d95f0ef9cf59" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247895 1034 "y=5x+1" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247895 1111 #uuid "104395e9-bd32-520c-b5d8-66522f7c9d31" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247896 1034 "y=-5x-5" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247896 1111 #uuid "0c6f6d0f-1dce-57c2-94e9-d8c74309b1cb" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247897 1034 "y=\\frac{5}{2}x+3" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247897 1111 #uuid "47deefea-56a7-5d71-a601-396a33834728" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247898 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-2" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247898 1111 #uuid "4c83a0b0-8eb4-57b7-a811-9be32bd14e78" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247900 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1089 "q-28338" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1100 "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+10y=20?$" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1113 #uuid "416f69f7-f0c9-5347-820e-f65417067948" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1138 false 13194139533710 true] [17592186247899 1145 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+10y=20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+10y & =20 \\\\ 10y & =-4x+20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{10}x+\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ has a slope of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line." 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247901 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247902 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247903 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247904 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247905 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1041 "selection" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247905 13194139533710 true] [17592186247900 1110 #uuid "74127ef1-65c1-5636-8d63-5380c9277829" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247901 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{16}{5}" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247901 1111 #uuid "7692d39b-6e2c-5006-a356-a468edaf0e29" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247902 1034 "y=\\frac{2}{5}x-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247902 1111 #uuid "44eb7e96-675a-5e84-bd27-c6f366e16099" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247903 1034 "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+4" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247903 1111 #uuid "c069330f-cec0-549b-96c4-8ae52a145061" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247904 1034 "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{12}{5}" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [17592186247904 1111 #uuid "cb84ce85-3e0b-5c4e-bf82-7d21458e846a" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247905 1034 "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4" 13194139533710 true] [17592186247905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533710 true] [1759218624790